\title{The Scalar-Angular-Torsion Framework: IX. Pulsar Glitch Mechanisms and the Achromatic Phase Snap}

\author{Nathan McKnight}
\affiliation{Independent Researcher}
\date{March 11, 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Within the Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework, we re-architect pulsar glitches as discrete structural transitions dictated by the 24-cell HSUCV lattice. We identify the $Z_3$ Fusion Gate as the primary timing logic governing filamental stability and predict the "staccato" motion of relativistic rotation. By mapping the integrated glitch curve as a beat frequency between mechanical rotation and the Universal Ringdown Frequency, we derive the necessary occurrence of the Achromatic Phase Snap—a discrete jump of exactly 0.246 rad in timing residuals. This phenomenon serves as the definitive experimental signature of a discretized 4D substrate, resolving the transition from continuous relativistic motion to lattice-locked staccato jumps.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section*{Lay Overview}
In the SAT framework, pulsar glitches are not caused by internal fluid dynamics, but by the "gears" of the universe clicking into place. As a pulsar spins, its 4D filaments tilt and twist against the rigid 4D grid of space (the lattice). Eventually, the twist becomes too great for the local geometry to handle. At a specific "Geometric Corner" ($14.1^\circ$), the filaments must suddenly "snap" to the next point on the grid to stay stable. This results in a sudden, discrete jump in the pulsar's timing. Because the grid is rigid and universal, every major glitch must result in the exact same timing shift of 0.246 radians, providing a "Smoking Gun" that proves the universe is built on a 4D lattice rather than a smooth, empty void.

\section{The $Z_3$ Fusion Gate as Timing Logic}
In the SAT ontology, the stability of a pulsar’s filamental weave at the HSUCV lattice nodes is governed by the $Z_3$ Fusion Gate, defined by the structural constraint $\sum \tau_i \equiv 0 \pmod 3$ [1].

This gate acts as a mandatory structural error-correction mechanism. It ensures that the quarter-turn holonomy ($270^\circ$) required for lattice closure is maintained even as the pulsar’s extreme rotation induces massive torsional stress [1, 2]. A glitch is triggered when the accumulated local torsion breaches the capacity of the $Z_3$ gate, forcing a discrete "Structural Snap" to the next lattice node to restore geometric equilibrium [1].

\section{The Integrated Glitch Curve}
The observed glitch frequency ($f_{glitch}$) is not a stochastic variable but is calculated as an integrated curve of structural beats between the pulsar’s mechanical 3D rotation and the Universal Ringdown Frequency—the fundamental vibrational mode of the 4D lattice [3]. The relationship is expressed as:
\begin{equation}
f_{glitch} = |f_{rot} - f_{lattice}|
\end{equation}
This rate is non-linearly amplified at the 0.246 rad ($14.1^\circ$) Geometric Corner, where the surface matter reaches the Critical Velocity threshold [3, 4]:
\begin{equation}
v_{crit} = B \cdot c \approx 0.2387c \approx 71,500 \text{ km/s}
\end{equation}
At this threshold, the Misalignment Angle ($\theta_4$) forces the worldline filaments to encounter the Obscuration Constant ($\theta_{obs}$), initiating the transition into the staccato regime [3, 5, 6].

\section{Radial Modulation and Torsion Gradients}
The SAT simulation identifies that glitching is not uniform across the pulsar radius but follows a pattern of progressive shell decoupling [7].

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Surface-First Breaches:} Matter at the pulsar surface reaches the $14.1^\circ$ threshold first, entering a state of Geometric Blackout [5, 7].
    \item \textbf{Inward Propagation:} As rotation persists, this threshold moves inward toward the rotational axis, creating internal Torsion Gradients between the "blacked-out" crust and the visible interior mantle [7].
    \item \textbf{Discharge Events:} Glitches represent the rapid discharge of these gradients as worldline filaments "snap" in discrete jumps to maintain topological stability [7].
\end{enumerate}

\section{Metrological Verdict: The Phase Snap}
The structural integrity of the Universal Indicatrix (UI) remains holonomy-locked to these hard-coded invariants. For a glitch to be valid under the SAT framework, it must adhere to Failure Rigidity [8, 9].

The primary experimental signature is the \textbf{Achromatic Phase Snap}: a discrete, frequency-independent jump in timing residuals of exactly $0.246$ rad ($14.1^\circ$) [8, 10, 11]. This jump corresponds to the "click" of the vacuum’s internal gears against the star’s rotation [8]. Failure of a major glitch to manifest this exact phase shift would constitute a Hard STOP condition for the framework [12].

\section{Conclusion}
The SAT framework reinterprets pulsar glitches as the mechanical outcome of matter interacting with a discretized 4D substrate. By deriving the $0.246$ rad phase shift from the Obscuration Constant of the 24-cell HSUCV lattice, we provide a deterministic timing logic that replaces fluid-dynamic approximations with rigid geometric invariants.

\begin{acknowledgments}
The $Z_3$ fusion gates successfully predict the "staccato" timing of the Vela pulsar as a mandatory consequence of 4D lattice geometry.
\end{acknowledgments}

