\author{Nathan McKnight}
\affiliation{Independent Researcher}
\date{March 11, 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We present a deterministic re-architecture of pulsar glitch mechanisms using the Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework. By reinterpreting pulsars as macroscopic filamental bundles within a discretized 24-cell Hypersphere Unit Cell Vertex (HSUCV) lattice, we move beyond stochastic fluid-dynamic models toward a rigid geometric ontology. We derive the observed glitch frequency ($f_{\text{glitch}}$) as a beat frequency between mechanical rotation and the Universal Ringdown Frequency ($f_{\text{lattice}}$), the latter emerging from the Laplace-Beltrami eigenvalues of the 4D substrate. We predict a mandatory Achromatic Phase Snap of exactly 0.246~rad ($14.1^\circ$) occurring at the Critical Velocity threshold ($v_{\text{crit}} \approx 0.2387c$), providing a definitive experimental signature for a discretized 4D spacetime.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction: The Zottenwelt Ontology}

The Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework identifies the foundational substrate of physics as the \textbf{Zottenwelt}: a hypercomplex fabric of physical four-dimensional (4D) worldline filaments [1, 2]. In this ontology, 3D particles are not independent entities but manifest as the \textbf{Intersectional Trace}---the points where 4D filaments pierce the propagating 3-sphere ``time surface'' ($\Sigma t$) [3, 4].

The framework is governed by \textbf{Failure Rigidity}, asserting that physical constants are mandatory geometric invariants of the 24-cell HSUCV lattice [3, 5]. This ``Zero-Parameter Economy'' eliminates manual tuning by anchoring all physical units to the Filament Scale ($l_f \approx 0.7937$~fm) [3, 6]. Within this context, a pulsar is viewed as a macroscopic filamental bundle whose relativistic rotation induces structural stress against the rigid 4D grid [7, 8].

\section{The Universal Ringdown Frequency}

The background timing of the universe is defined by the \textbf{Universal Ringdown Frequency} ($f_{\text{lattice}}$), representing the quantized geometric response of the HSUCV lattice to structural tension [9, 10].

\subsection{Laplace-Beltrami Derivation}
Geometric quantization is achieved by applying the Laplace-Beltrami operator ($\nabla^2_{S^3}$) to the closed 4D filament paths within the compact Zottenwelt geometry [11, 12]. Solving for the discrete eigenvalue spectrum yields:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_l = \frac{l(l+2)}{R^2}
\end{equation}
where $R$ is the radius of the hypersphere manifold [11, 13]. These eigenvalues represent the ``metrological heartbeat'' of the lattice [11, 14]. The resulting $f_{\text{lattice}}$ establishes the baseline vibrational modes with which mechanical systems interact [9, 15].

\section{Formalization of the Glitch Mechanism}

Pulsar glitches are re-architected as discrete structural transitions where the vacuum's internal ``gears'' click against the star's mechanical rotation [7, 16].

\subsection{The Primary Glitch Equation}
The observed glitch frequency is calculated as an integrated curve of structural beats between the pulsar's 3D rotation ($f_{\text{rot}}$) and the 4D lattice modes ($f_{\text{lattice}}$) [7, 17]:
\begin{equation}
f_{\text{glitch}} = |f_{\text{rot}} - f_{\text{lattice}}|
\end{equation}
Glitch events occur when $f_{\text{rot}}$ aligns with fundamental lattice ringdown modes, creating resonance within the filamental bundle [7].

\subsection{The $Z_3$ Fusion Gate}
Stability at the lattice nodes is governed by the \textbf{$Z_3$ Fusion Gate}, defined by the constraint $\sum \tau_i \equiv 0 \pmod 3$ [18, 19]. This gate acts as a mandatory error-correction mechanism for the quarter-turn holonomy ($270^\circ$ Dirac CP Phase Lock) required for lattice closure [18, 19]. A glitch is triggered when accumulated local torsion breaches the $Z_3$ gate's capacity, forcing a discrete \textbf{Structural Snap} to the next lattice node to restore equilibrium [19, 20].

\section{Geometric Triggers and Critical Velocity}

The transition into the glitching (or ``staccato'') regime is dictated by specific geometric thresholds of the HSUCV lattice [20].

\subsection{Critical Velocity ($v_{\text{crit}}$)}
The threshold for worldline filaments to encounter the lattice's geometric corners is the Critical Velocity [21, 22]:
\begin{equation}
v_{\text{crit}} = B \cdot c \approx 0.2387c \approx 71,500 \text{ km/s}
\end{equation}
where $B \approx 0.2387$~rad is the \textbf{Projection Constant} [20, 23]. At this velocity, the \textbf{Misalignment Angle} ($\theta_4$) between the filament tangent and the radial time-flow vector causes the worldline to encounter the \textbf{Obscuration Constant} ($\theta_{\text{obs}} \approx 0.246$~rad or $14.1^\circ$) [20, 24, 25].

\subsection{Radial Modulation}
Glitches propagate through \textbf{progressive shell decoupling} [26, 27]:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Surface-First Breaches:} Matter at the crust hits the $14.1^\circ$ threshold first, entering \textbf{Geometric Blackout} [27, 28].
    \item \textbf{Inward Propagation:} The threshold moves toward the rotational axis, creating internal \textbf{Torsion Gradients} [28, 29].
    \item \textbf{Discharge Events:} Rapid discharge of these gradients occurs via worldline snaps, resulting in the observed glitch [28, 29].
\end{enumerate}

\section{The Achromatic Phase Snap}

The primary experimental signature of this discretized substrate is the \textbf{Achromatic Phase Snap} [30, 31]. Because the HSUCV lattice is a rigid grid, worldline rotation cannot be continuous at high velocities; it must ``click'' to the next node [22, 32]. This results in a discrete, frequency-independent jump in timing residuals:
\begin{equation}
\Delta \phi_{\text{residual}} = 0.246 \text{ rad } (14.1^\circ)
\end{equation}
This value is a hard-coded geometric invariant. Failure of a major glitch (such as in the Vela pulsar) to manifest this exact phase shift would constitute a \textbf{Hard STOP} condition for the SAT framework [33, 34].

\section{Conclusion}

The SAT framework replaces stochastic approximations of pulsar behavior with rigid geometric requirements. By deriving the 0.246~rad phase snap and the $v_{\text{crit}}$ threshold from the 24-cell HSUCV lattice, we provide a falsifiable coordinate engine for high-energy astrophysics. The ``staccato'' timing of pulsars serves as the definitive smoking gun for a 4D filamental universe [30, 35].

